{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1 and sign node1's SSH host key with it.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A new ED25519 CA was created, and the resulting host certificate is explicitly identified as a host certificate signed by that CA, with principals for node1 and 10.100.228.10.","evidence_ids":["cmd-341b6cbc3d564c68","cmd-5691892ff85546b4"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Configure node1's SSH server to present the CA-signed host certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective sshd configuration references the certificate, and a live key scan retrieved that same signed certificate from node1:22. Post-restart evidence confirms sshd continued offering it.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d451eeaccbd34473","cmd-18d6078b6fbb495c","cmd-bd696281bcd54b84"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Demonstrate that a client trusting only the CA can connect without pinning node1's individual host key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The test known-hosts file contained only one @cert-authority entry. Strict host checking matched the presented host certificate to that CA and the SSH connection succeeded. The same test also succeeded after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-69aec16d436a4c24","cmd-76cdfd17cc1845b1","cmd-5f060596697146e5"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary state-changing mutations were identified. Creating the CA, certificate, and sshd drop-in and reloading sshd directly implemented the task (cmd-341b6cbc3d564c68, cmd-5691892ff85546b4, cmd-d451eeaccbd34473, cmd-18d6078b6fbb495c). Temporary client credentials, authorization, and CA-only trust material were necessary for bounded behavioral testing and were subsequently removed (cmd-69aec16d436a4c24, cmd-8bf08694697b40c6). The reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol and produced a changed boot identity with recovered SSH service (cmd-f9cddc0854534fe9, cmd-e4b5d6a8acf04312, cmd-bd696281bcd54b84). Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, no additional exposed sockets, healthy package state, and no task-created temporary-file residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-341b6cbc3d564c68","cmd-5691892ff85546b4","cmd-d451eeaccbd34473","cmd-18d6078b6fbb495c","cmd-69aec16d436a4c24","cmd-8bf08694697b40c6","cmd-f9cddc0854534fe9","cmd-e4b5d6a8acf04312","cmd-bd696281bcd54b84","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct configuration and behavioral evidence. The mandatory restart protocol is also complete: node1 was healthy before restart, the restart was acknowledged, its boot identity changed from d4784d1b-162e-4faa-b581-f6427617d23c to 0bb4663a-be51-409f-90d7-e8dc32574a9b, and sshd, its listener, the signed certificate, and CA-trusted connectivity recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the identity change.","limitations":["The cleanup command directly removed the temporary authorized-key entry, but its printed cleanup check was malformed and did not independently display the resulting authorized_keys contents. No contrary evidence or residual temporary test files were observed."]}
